Telegram's `reply_markup`, exactly as the Bot API spells it — passed through verbatim (no wrapping, no typed shape here; a malformed keyboard surfaces as a loud Telegram error at run time). Usually an inline keyboard: rows of buttons, each `{ text, url }` (opens a link) or `{ text, callback_data }` (a tap this bot handles, capped by Telegram at 64 BYTES). To make a tap DO something, mint a callback (`c = callback({ … })`) and put `"${c.id}"` in `callback_data` — it fits the 64-byte cap comfortably — one callback per button, since its body is what that button means. Telegram captures nothing at tap time, so a callback fired from a keyboard takes no values; anything a person has to fill in needs a `url` button instead. Buttons compose with everything else on the send. Build strings with "${…}" interpolation (never `+`, which is numeric addition). Example:
{ inline_keyboard: [
  [ { text: "Approve", callback_data: "${approve.id}" },
    { text: "Decline", callback_data: "${decline.id}" } ],
] }

